#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128979 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "2f8542a8-1e9e-56f8-9eae-e5b4fb592d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5965" :question/problem "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "6a7316fa-77fe-584f-bd92-a37521371482" :question/math-academy-id "e-5966" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz & =1 \\\\ xyz & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the surface $xyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k,$ where $k$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "397d8dee-c35f-5908-baa6-002e09dab477" :question/math-academy-id "e-5967" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474ec144-400e-50ee-acad-dceee080baaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260494} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6d573a-d40c-5d85-80e3-7764d33101a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260523} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef7db89-4af8-5e81-82ab-8aeba1e3aff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260851} {:answer/id #uuid "7da2ea3b-8bd6-5179-9757-6a8f6fd51909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260969} {:answer/id #uuid "a94945f9-841e-5252-97e1-1393c01bef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "474ec144-400e-50ee-acad-dceee080baaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260494} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae38095-1f0f-55b2-9542-9dd1b06eab3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7851585d-95a0-58bf-8b24-146b7e54a05e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129186" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1 & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2add1d4-43d1-5f4f-9cf4-3c57efe83bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375911} {:answer/id #uuid "87928515-562e-5e86-9ae1-75c93c36a9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375912} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dfc0c9-e865-5bdf-afd0-790efb23d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375913} {:answer/id #uuid "3828f0b1-a195-5142-ba1a-628f13dca094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375914} {:answer/id #uuid "19eefaaf-3e19-5278-ab21-7022f28cf228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5dfc0c9-e865-5bdf-afd0-790efb23d68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375913} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbed5460-d016-5ddc-a1f1-08c464f3a5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92aca500-bcb6-5db4-b189-6a2dd54dd3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-129188" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{z}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{z}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=4$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55494500-715b-5b92-bdc3-10be6037589a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380756} {:answer/id #uuid "76f0fd5e-c88a-542a-b015-5ae251d467f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380757} {:answer/id #uuid "62414edb-89fd-5da4-be1e-8d5ce90959bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380758} {:answer/id #uuid "138f787a-24ee-54da-ae09-a5ee644089db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380759} {:answer/id #uuid "1068b75f-04e1-59f1-a1bf-a73dc1a39633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1068b75f-04e1-59f1-a1bf-a73dc1a39633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0499e430-8eb2-5056-a920-a8a94029c1ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f1ebfbb-47b8-501d-a8ba-4b135dec1a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129190" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=x-2y+z$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,-1) & =1-2(1)+(-1) \\\\  & =1-2-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ x-2y+z & =-2 \\\\ x-2y+z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,-1)$ is the plane $x-2y+z+2=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3873cd-b42b-554b-871d-bc57c2e46dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260126} {:answer/id #uuid "59b37513-91b4-5452-8039-f1442ebbe9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260641} {:answer/id #uuid "65e428b6-3228-5fc4-94a4-e09746734ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260759} {:answer/id #uuid "80604487-c7d5-5952-a43f-dc6534079ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260990} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e53784-c85b-5c3f-ad9a-fceab86ac769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b37513-91b4-5452-8039-f1442ebbe9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260641} :answer-field/id #uuid "00bca234-7264-515a-b29d-57c969a28d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9afcbb8-174d-59c8-9dca-bfd7bbc4e0d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129192" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x-y+z-1}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1-1+1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{x-y+z-1} & =1 \\\\ x-y+z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,1)$ is the plane $x-y+z-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f39980-6ad0-53d7-ad63-fbb34a382f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259949} {:answer/id #uuid "1456f86c-f369-5c8a-aa73-f917e987fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic cones: ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260057} {:answer/id #uuid "64d0fc97-78ec-5077-acfa-96dd790ebb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260745} {:answer/id #uuid "9b989c09-44c2-5eab-8bb7-5a6b7ceb875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=x+3y+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261222} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c9051c-074f-5a24-a621-b2a3449ab493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07f39980-6ad0-53d7-ad63-fbb34a382f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259949} :answer-field/id #uuid "653d007f-928b-501b-88fc-8c02a0e38f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9849d588-2a95-5d11-993e-dd1a8d5d4837" :question/math-academy-id "q-129196" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3 & =c \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+c-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let $c-3=C,$ we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C,\\,C\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf1505a-2ffb-54f0-8f3f-5ea33a577c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259986} {:answer/id #uuid "404cb377-65f5-556c-ae34-190f19017c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260438} {:answer/id #uuid "82fab96e-d95f-5b58-94b5-d5f6e6b18844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261010} {:answer/id #uuid "9401ed06-d4ea-5a07-b8c8-da7fe7bc1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261163} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf8e9b9-6466-54b9-80d7-0e77dbe93073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "404cb377-65f5-556c-ae34-190f19017c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260438} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d33b4f-f380-5ade-9104-82d536559bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81f83dd7-0d5e-5e99-af82-480e47d0d33e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129203" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c \\\\ z & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of curves:\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c},\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe7a136-a085-5af5-8227-8a1c165d5330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382161} {:answer/id #uuid "04bc1f8c-1194-5363-8f77-7b794aa83d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)+2{e}^{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382162} {:answer/id #uuid "93b65ce9-9436-5a83-905b-f5f0a55ba0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+2{e}^{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382163} {:answer/id #uuid "6c64d55e-70be-5bb7-afc9-729f0d37f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382164} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd4df73-680e-5e48-820a-072700a31ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)-\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c64d55e-70be-5bb7-afc9-729f0d37f4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382164} :answer-field/id #uuid "47eae618-f07d-53d0-87a8-6feeb55bab75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f45199e-f2f3-5936-bade-aabcd5824521" :question/math-academy-id "q-71577" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}{{e}^{x-y}},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}{{e}^{x-y}} & =-2 \\\\ z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-2$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2{e}^{x-y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f390ba1-08ec-564f-b857-a06ee4442090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382175} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d5fa4c-e1bd-52fa-b4d7-ccef48b5995a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382176} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3db37f-4c6d-511f-914a-4e10eb02c1fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382177} {:answer/id #uuid "76262752-1d95-5ef1-a777-a131d3b64059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382178} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f6220c-93a4-5ad1-a509-4c8620c4b55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f390ba1-08ec-564f-b857-a06ee4442090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382175} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a741eee-71ac-5a98-a484-c60f3657f2da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "636f6d0d-a3f8-5d3d-b5aa-3ee795351d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71595" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)-\\sqrt{xy}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot e)-\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)-\\sqrt{xy} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz) & =\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ xyz & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,e)$ is the surface $z=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{xy}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341c06af-d1ba-5272-bb61-52ad376125d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336522} {:answer/id #uuid "5700ae9e-968d-5bb9-b16c-fb2495caa581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336830} {:answer/id #uuid "726140df-5488-5e91-b878-03e0a7dc18a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337057} {:answer/id #uuid "caa01bba-d8dc-529e-84eb-7a172fdebb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337815} {:answer/id #uuid "f1709cb8-4e61-5ab7-a2f4-a614c896f494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa01bba-d8dc-529e-84eb-7a172fdebb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337815} :answer-field/id #uuid "8165f0ea-43de-5434-a42c-9e10144a7415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e38a7de1-ef3c-50e7-9539-8728abb34f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-71597" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,22).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,22) & =22-4(2)^{2}-6(1)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,22)$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}.$"}]}
